<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Transport security for the one server of ours the app talks to: the link
  guard's signed site list on lists.skycoax.uz. The scanners themselves send
  nothing anywhere, so there is no scanner API host here.

  Reference it from the fork's AndroidManifest:

      <application android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/jac_network_security_config" ...>

  Note this file governs the WHOLE app, Telegram's own MTProto sockets
  included. That is fine — MTProto is not HTTP and is unaffected by
  cleartextTrafficPermitted — but it does mean this file must never be
  loosened for our convenience.

  Two layers, and the reason for each:

  1. cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" at the base. On API 28+ this is already
     the platform default, so the value here is for older devices and, more
     usefully, for making the intent explicit and reviewable. HttpBackendClient
     independently refuses a non-https base URL, so a mistake has to get past
     two unrelated checks.

  2. A certificate-pin set for the API domain. This is the layer that matters
     in this region: the threat is not a self-signed certificate, it is a CA
     that will issue for our domain to someone who is not us, whether through
     compromise or instruction. Public CA trust alone does not survive that;
     a pin does.

  PROVISIONING — this file ships WITHOUT a pin-set, on purpose.

  Publishing a wrong pin is worse than publishing none: it bricks the API for
  every installed client with no server-side fix and no way to reach the
  users, because the update mechanism is the very channel a pin failure
  blocks.

  AND — learned the hard way — a pin-set with placeholder text is not a
  harmless reminder, it is a time bomb. Android parses this ENTIRE file the
  first time anything in the process touches the platform network stack (first
  HTTPS connection, first cleartext-policy check, WebView init). A pin whose
  text is not valid base64, or does not decode to exactly 32 bytes for
  SHA-256, makes that parse throw; the framework wraps it in a RuntimeException
  on whichever thread asked first, and the process dies. Symptom: the app
  installs, shows the splash, and exits about a second later — but only on
  devices where something makes an in-process HTTPS request early (a real
  phone), not on an emulator that happens never to leave MTProto. One bad pin
  poisons connections to ALL hosts, not just the pinned domain. So: no
  placeholders in this file, ever. The pin-set is ADDED at provisioning time,
  not filled in.

  Before release:

    1. Set the domain to your API host.
    2. Compute the SPKI pin of your LEAF certificate:

         openssl s_client -servername api.example.uz -connect api.example.uz:443 \
           | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout \
           | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
           | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | base64

    3. Compute a SECOND pin from a backup key that is NOT yet deployed and is
       held offline. Two pins is not belt-and-braces; it is the only way to
       rotate a certificate without bricking every installed client, and
       Android will refuse the connection if the presented chain matches
       neither.
    4. Set expiration to the date the current certificate expires. After that
       date pinning stops being enforced rather than failing closed — which is
       the correct trade for a mechanism whose failure mode is permanent and
       unfixable, and it is why the date must be kept current.

  tools/preflight.mjs fails a release build while the placeholders are here.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!--
      Cleartext stays PERMITTED at the base, and that is not an oversight.

      This app is also a web browser. Upstream sets
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" precisely so a tapped http:// link
      opens instead of failing, and a lot of the region's sites are still
      plain http. Forbidding cleartext app-wide would break opening those
      links — a visible, everyday regression, traded for no security gain,
      because the user chose to visit that page and we already warned them
      about it if it was suspicious.

      What must never be cleartext is OUR server, and that is handled by the
      domain-config below. Locking down the one download the app makes from
      us is the whole point; locking down the browser is a different product
      decision that would make the app worse at being a messenger.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!--
      User-installed CAs are deliberately NOT trusted for our server. On a
      device where someone has installed an interception certificate — an
      employer's MDM profile, or an app the user was talked into installing —
      whoever put it there could otherwise stand in for it.
    -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <!--
          The link guard's signed site list (LinkGuard). The file is public and
          the ECDSA signature inside it is what the app trusts, so this entry
          is not what keeps a forged list out. It is here so the download can
          never go over cleartext or through a user-installed CA: neither could
          forge a list, but either could replay an old signed one to a fresh
          install, or hold a phone on a stale list while looking like a working
          server. Exact host only, and no pin-set, for the reason below.

          Android reads this file from 7.0 on. On 5.0 to 7.0 the same two
          promises are kept in code: LinkGuard refuses a non-https list URL,
          and LinkGuardTrust builds that one connection's trust from the
          system CAs only (plus the ISRG roots those versions lack).
        -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">lists.skycoax.uz</domain>
        <!--
          NO pin-set yet — pinning was deliberately deferred. Until real pins
          exist this domain-config contributes only the cleartext refusal,
          which is worth having on its own.

          Do NOT put a pin-set with placeholder values here as a reminder: an
          unparseable pin kills the whole app at its first network touch (see
          the header comment). When provisioning for real, uncomment and fill:

            <pin-set expiration="(cert expiry date, e.g. 2027-08-01)">
                <pin digest="SHA-256">(leaf SPKI pin, base64, 32 bytes)</pin>
                <pin digest="SHA-256">(offline backup key pin, base64)</pin>
            </pin-set>

          Note for whoever provisions these: the certificate must NOT be
          proxied through Cloudflare when you take the pin. Behind an orange
          cloud the app sees Cloudflare's certificate, not the server's, so the
          pin would bind us to a key we do not control and would break the day
          Cloudflare rotates it. The DNS record is currently "DNS only", which
          is the correct setting for this reason.
        -->
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </domain-config>

    <!--
      Localhost over cleartext, debug builds only. Overlays are ignored
      entirely in a release build, so this cannot leak into production even if
      someone forgets it is here.
    -->
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>

</network-security-config>
